jadx only decompiles Dalvik bytecode (.dex files) into Java/Kotlin, so it cannot decompile .so (Shared Object) native libraries. These .so files are compiled C/C++ binaries (ELF format) located inside the APK's lib/ folder, which require a dedicated native disassembler or decompiler.

1. How to Extract .so Files from the APK

Since an APK is just a standard ZIP archive, you can pull the native libraries out directly without waiting for jadx to finish:
bash
unzip Wolt.apk "lib/*" -d wolt_native_libs
Use code with caution.
• Architecture folders: Inside wolt_native_libs/lib/, you will usually see folders like arm64-v8a, armeabi-v7a, or x86_64. Focus on arm64-v8a, as it is the standard 64-bit architecture for modern Android devices and generally produces cleaner decompilation output.

2. Tools to Inspect and Decompile .so Files

Once extracted, use one of the following tools depending on how deep you need to look:
• Ghidra (Best Free Decompiler): Developed by the NSA, Ghidra disassembles ARM64/x86 binaries and decompiles them back into readable C-like pseudocode. You can install it on Ubuntu and import the .so file directly into a new project.
• Radare2 / Cutter: Cutter is a free, open-source GUI powered by Radare2 that offers fast disassembly, string searching, and decompilation via the Ghidra decompiler plugin.
• Quick Command-Line Inspection: If you only want to check exported functions, linked libraries, or hardcoded strings without opening a heavy GUI, use standard Linux binary utilities:
	• Check strings: strings -n 6 libexample.so | less
	• List exported JNI functions: nm -D libexample.so | grep Java_ or readelf -Ws libexample.so

3. Understanding How Java Connects to .so

• JNI Bridges: Look in your jadx output for methods marked with the native keyword (e.g., public native String getSignature();) and calls to System.loadLibrary("name").
• Matching Functions: A Java method like com.wolt.app.NativeHelper.init() maps to a C function named Java_com_wolt_app_NativeHelper_init inside libname.so, unless it is registered dynamically inside JNI_OnLoad.
